## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where all CMS content is resolved and provided to the React tree. Currently it only passes through static defaultContent from getStaticProps (build-time filesystem cache). For Contentstack Live Preview to work, this provider must detect preview mode and fetch content live from the Contentstack Preview API instead of using the baked-in static content. Every component downstream (via useContent) gets its data from here, so enabling live preview here propagates to the entire app.
  - **Minimal fix:** In ContentstackProvider, detect Contentstack Live Preview mode (check for live_preview hash/cookie in window location or Next.js preview context). When in preview mode, use the Contentstack SDK's livePreviewQuery to fetch content directly from the Contentstack Preview API and store it in state (useState/useEffect) instead of using the static defaultContent. The provider should accept a contentstack client or preview config via props, and when preview is active, override the static content with live-fetched content that updates on each preview change. The IContentStackProviderProps interface needs a new optional field for the Contentstack SDK client instance (or preview config) so getStaticProps can pass it through.
- **src/pages/[[...slug]].tsx** (`getStaticProps`): This is the main page entry point that assembles all content from the filesystem cache (readFromFs) and passes it as props to ContentstackProvider. For Live Preview, this function must also detect Next.js preview mode (context.preview) and when active, pass the Contentstack SDK client or preview configuration through to ContentstackProvider so it can fetch live content instead of using the cached data.
  - **Minimal fix:** When context.preview is true (Next.js preview mode), pass a contentstackPreview flag or SDK client instance through props alongside the static content, so ContentstackProvider knows to activate live preview fetching. The static content can still be passed as fallback. This requires adding the preview config to IPageProps and threading it through to ContentstackProvider.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a tester opens a page via its preview link, the page renders successfully in the browser rather than showing an error page or a stale build.
- With preview mode active, the page displays the current live content from the CMS, including entries or edits that are not present in the last published build.
- When content is edited and saved in the CMS while the preview page is open, the preview page reflects the change without the tester performing a full page reload.
- After a CMS edit, the on-screen components that render that content — headings, body text, and images from the edited entry — display the updated values.
- A tester can confirm live preview end-to-end in a standard browser by making an edit in the CMS and observing the preview page update, with no test harness, mocks, or code inspection required.
- When the same page is visited outside preview mode, it continues to serve the normal published static content unchanged.

